filipinos tuned in to president rodrigo  duterte's presumably  last state of the nation speech on  monday with critics looking for clues to  see whether he'll actually give up power  his term is supposed to wrap up next  year the populist leader has  chipped away at the country's democratic  institutions yet has maintained  tremendous support from the majority of  citizens  he has however vocal opponents  [Music]  these protesters can't wait to see the  back of their president  [Applause]  before rodrigo duterte's speech in  congress  demonstrators thronged the streets of  manila  calling for him to step down  beyond the police cordon duterte arrived  for his final state of the nation  address  for nearly three and a half hours the  president talked tough  renewing his often repeated threat to  kill drug dealers  those who destroy my country  i will kill you and those  who destroy the young people of  our country i will kill you  because i love my country  duterte's term and offers ends in june  next year  but his opponents fear that he will find  a way to hold on to power  the ruling party has already called for  him to stand as vice president  possibly with his daughter running to  succeed him  duterte is the one forcing us to protest  during the pandemic  duterte wants to stay in power for  another six years and i think  that is the single biggest threat to the  country right now he's staying in power  beyond 2022.  so he's the one pushing people to  protest his policies are pushing people  to protest  those policies include duterte's  notorious war on drugs  in which thousands have been killed  since he came to power in 2016  such as the scale of the purges that the  international criminal court is  considering investigating duterte  for crimes against humanity  the covet pandemic has also tested  duterte's presidency  lockdowns have plunged the philippines  into the worst recession since world war  ii and the government's vaccination  campaign has faced delays due to supply  problems  with just over five percent of filipinos  fully vaccinated  and the delta variant sending infection  numbers up once again  the pandemic is set to dominate  duterte's agenda  in the last year of his presidency  joining us is nicole corrado from the  center for deliberative democracy and  global governance at the university of  canberra nicole i think at the top of  everyone's mind  is duterte turning out of his presidency  but no one seems to believe he'll give  up power  what is the prospect he'll find a way to  stay on  well one possibility that he raised is  to run again  for vice president the philippines has  uh termix us or maximum terms for  presidency so he's finding a legal  loophole  to still remain in power without  violating the constitution  so if he is going to  do that what are we going to see in  terms of the durability of the  philippines democracy i mean we're three  decades  out since the people power revolution  that ended the marco's dictatorship in  1986.  yes and i think what's at stake here is  not just philippine democracy it's also  the strength of global institutions  that try to protect liberal democracy so  one reason why president duterte wants  to stay on in power is to make sure that  he has immunity  from the prosecution of the  international criminal court  so last december icc chief prosecutor  fatou bensouda said that the scale of  deaths in the philippines related to the  drug war  is proportionate to crimes against  humanity and obviously president duterte  is implicated here so it's not just the  philippines democracy that it's at stake  here  president duterte is testing the  boundary and the power  of global institutions that hold leaders  accountable around the world  you talk about global institutions but  i'm curious about the  domestic institutions uh how durable are  they  the different branches of the government  to ensure that despite  someone with autocratic tendencies like  duterte that  uh the philippines democracy can endure  so one of the concerning institutions  that's very much corroded in the duterte  administration  are the coercive apparatus particularly  the police  so we see a generation of new policemen  who basically have been trained in the  drug war of being able to kill  on citizens with impunity without due  process so that is definitely  worth monitoring there have also been  concerns that the supreme court has been  heavily politicized  and in the congress he holds a super  majority so what are we seeing here the  basic checks and balances  in the philippines has been corroded in  the duterte administration  and yet he still remains legitimate  because more than 90 percent of  filipinos  give him a high satisfaction rating so  it sounds like  what duterte said in his speech he  invoked his war on drugs which of course  has led to extrajudicial killings  and condemnation from human rights  groups  that people are still buying into his  talk and  and what i don't understand is the  pandemic seems to not have changed too  much of that there's still a lot of  support for him  yes although i do have to qualify that  so my research for the past  five years has been to understand the  kind of support that ordinary filipinos  give to president duterte  and i must say that it's not fanatical  support it's very much a conditional  support  so if you ask ordinary filipinos whether  in surveys or in  in-depth interviews a lot of them would  say that they expected more from the  government  but a lot of them also don't necessarily  blame president duterte for the  philippines condition  in relation to the pandemic a recent  survey actually found that ordinary  citizens  blame their fellow ordinary citizens who  are violating health protocols  so in a way it's kind of easy to  understand why the president still  maintains popularity  blame is not assigned to him but blame  is assigned to  fellow citizens which is the same logic  that we see when we think about the drug  war  when you ask ordinary filipinos about  the drug war  they would say actually yes i feel safer  in the streets now  but they also recognized that they or  their family members could be the next  victim  of violence or extrajudicial killings so  it's a bit more complicated than it's  typically portrayed by international  media  ordinary filipinos who support the  president are not are not fanatical  it's a product of weighing and different  considerations  uh that make them support the president  now we seem to see  a lot of countries in the region tilting  away from democracy  to autocracy thailand myanmar how  concerning  is all of that including of course the  philippines  and putting it all in context  yes it's definitely concerning the  philippines is one of asia's oldest  democracy  it has given itself pride by having some  of the freest media  around the world but what we are seeing  as part of the duterte legacy is the  encroachment of those freedoms so i  think the elections in 2022 will be a  big test  because either we see a restoration of  some form of trust for democracy  or we might see an authoritarian  consolidation  nicole corrado thank you so much for  joining us  my pleasure  you
 

